1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:02,940
On the evening of December 25th,

2
00:00:03,060 --> 00:00:07,101
1991, the red flag over the Kremlin came

3
00:00:07,142 --> 00:00:08,322
down for the last time.

4
00:00:08,862 --> 00:00:11,163
Within a day, the largest country on Earth

5
00:00:11,223 --> 00:00:13,983
was gone, and in its place stood fifteen

6
00:00:14,063 --> 00:00:14,703
new nations.

7
00:00:15,144 --> 00:00:16,684
But here is the strange part.

8
00:00:17,104 --> 00:00:19,665
The Soviet Union was not built from fifteen

9
00:00:19,745 --> 00:00:20,165
peoples.

10
00:00:20,505 --> 00:00:22,605
It was a patchwork of more than a hundred

11
00:00:22,665 --> 00:00:25,286
nationalities, organized into fifty-three

12
00:00:25,466 --> 00:00:26,546
official homelands.

13
00:00:26,906 --> 00:00:29,207
Tatarstan had more than twice as many people

14
00:00:29,287 --> 00:00:31,748
as Estonia, yet Estonia became a country

15
00:00:32,128 --> 00:00:33,308
and Tatarstan did not.

16
00:00:33,688 --> 00:00:36,149
Chechnya would fight two brutal wars over

17
00:00:36,229 --> 00:00:38,970
independence, and end up back under Moscow's

18
00:00:39,030 --> 00:00:39,470
control.

19
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:42,751
So why fifteen, and not twenty,

20
00:00:43,191 --> 00:00:44,051
or fifty?

21
00:00:44,591 --> 00:00:46,671
The answer had been written into the Soviet

22
00:00:46,732 --> 00:00:49,792
system decades earlier, by Lenin and Stalin

23
00:00:49,812 --> 00:00:50,432
themselves.

24
00:00:50,833 --> 00:00:53,153
This is why the Soviet Union broke into

25
00:00:53,233 --> 00:00:54,974
exactly fifteen countries.

26
00:00:58,024 --> 00:01:00,184
When the Bolsheviks won the Russian Civil

27
00:01:00,244 --> 00:01:02,104
War, they inherited a problem.

28
00:01:02,464 --> 00:01:04,505
The old Russian Empire had been a prison

29
00:01:04,625 --> 00:01:07,025
of nations, and many of those nations had

30
00:01:07,105 --> 00:01:08,305
briefly broken free.

31
00:01:08,925 --> 00:01:11,645
Lenin's solution was a clever compromise.

32
00:01:12,165 --> 00:01:15,386
Ironically, Stalin, then in charge of nationality

33
00:01:15,466 --> 00:01:18,206
affairs, wanted every republic to simply

34
00:01:18,346 --> 00:01:20,886
join Russia as autonomous republics.

35
00:01:21,486 --> 00:01:24,306
Lenin overruled him and insisted on a union

36
00:01:24,427 --> 00:01:26,187
of supposedly equal partners.

37
00:01:26,647 --> 00:01:30,767
On December 30th, 1922, four Soviet republics,

38
00:01:31,127 --> 00:01:34,267
Russia, Ukraine, Belarus and Transcaucasia,

39
00:01:34,707 --> 00:01:37,068
approved a treaty creating the Union of

40
00:01:37,188 --> 00:01:38,768
Soviet Socialist Republics.

41
00:01:39,388 --> 00:01:42,168
On paper, it was a voluntary union of equal

42
00:01:42,228 --> 00:01:42,708
nations.

43
00:01:43,128 --> 00:01:45,248
In reality, it was a matryoshka,

44
00:01:45,669 --> 00:01:46,849
a Russian nesting doll.

45
00:01:47,269 --> 00:01:49,629
At the top stood the union republics.

46
00:01:50,089 --> 00:01:53,249
Inside them sat autonomous republics.

47
00:01:53,909 --> 00:01:55,609
Then came autonomous regions,

48
00:01:55,889 --> 00:01:57,690
and smaller autonomous districts,

49
00:01:58,090 --> 00:02:00,370
each with fewer rights than the layer above.

50
00:02:00,810 --> 00:02:04,250
By 1989, there were fifteen union republics,

51
00:02:04,610 --> 00:02:06,350
twenty autonomous republics,

52
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:09,891
eight autonomous regions and ten autonomous

53
00:02:09,971 --> 00:02:10,471
districts.

54
00:02:11,111 --> 00:02:12,951
Every layer had its own borders,

55
00:02:13,351 --> 00:02:16,511
its own capital, and its own official nationality.

56
00:02:17,432 --> 00:02:18,932
But only the outer shell,

57
00:02:19,412 --> 00:02:22,792
the union republics, held one extraordinary

58
00:02:23,132 --> 00:02:23,632
privilege.

59
00:02:24,212 --> 00:02:26,832
The Soviet constitution guaranteed each

60
00:02:26,892 --> 00:02:29,593
of them the right to freely leave the Union.

61
00:02:30,233 --> 00:02:33,133
For decades, that right meant nothing.

62
00:02:34,113 --> 00:02:36,813
Any real attempt to use it would have ended

63
00:02:36,973 --> 00:02:37,853
in a prison camp.

64
00:02:38,233 --> 00:02:39,454
But the words were there,

65
00:02:40,014 --> 00:02:41,474
written in black and white,

66
00:02:41,954 --> 00:02:42,314
waiting.

67
00:02:43,233 --> 00:02:46,073
By the mid-1930s, the map had already grown.

68
00:02:46,494 --> 00:02:49,014
Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan were created

69
00:02:49,094 --> 00:02:52,874
in 1924 and joined the Union in 1925,

70
00:02:53,135 --> 00:02:55,255
Tajikistan in 1929.

71
00:02:55,715 --> 00:03:00,076
Then, in 1936, Stalin unveiled a new constitution.

72
00:03:00,436 --> 00:03:02,856
Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan were promoted

73
00:03:02,896 --> 00:03:05,516
to full union republics, and the Transcaucasian

74
00:03:05,556 --> 00:03:07,377
republic was split into Georgia,

75
00:03:07,737 --> 00:03:09,357
Armenia and Azerbaijan.

76
00:03:09,737 --> 00:03:10,877
That made eleven.

77
00:03:11,217 --> 00:03:13,278
When the draft constitution was debated,

78
00:03:13,638 --> 00:03:16,078
a proposal to let autonomous republics be

79
00:03:16,158 --> 00:03:18,718
promoted as they developed was rejected,

80
00:03:19,058 --> 00:03:21,579
and Stalin explained the rules in a famous

81
00:03:21,639 --> 00:03:22,019
speech.

82
00:03:22,639 --> 00:03:24,319
To become a union republic,

83
00:03:24,699 --> 00:03:26,340
a nation needed three things.

84
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:29,820
First, it had to lie on the outer border

85
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:31,200
of the Soviet Union.

86
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:33,421
After all, Stalin argued,

87
00:03:33,741 --> 00:03:36,661
a republic surrounded on every side by Soviet

88
00:03:36,721 --> 00:03:39,142
territory could never actually leave.

89
00:03:39,722 --> 00:03:42,542
Second, its namesake nationality had to

90
00:03:42,622 --> 00:03:44,382
form a compact majority.

91
00:03:45,843 --> 00:03:48,403
And third, its population had to be large

92
00:03:48,443 --> 00:03:50,483
enough, more than a million people.

93
00:03:50,863 --> 00:03:52,504
Now look at Tatarstan.

94
00:03:52,944 --> 00:03:56,484
By 1989, it had about three point six million

95
00:03:56,544 --> 00:03:59,205
people, while Estonia had about one point

96
00:03:59,265 --> 00:03:59,845
six million.

97
00:04:00,345 --> 00:04:02,625
A proud history, its own language,

98
00:04:02,905 --> 00:04:04,725
but it sits deep inside Russia,

99
00:04:05,045 --> 00:04:07,086
hundreds of kilometers from any foreign

100
00:04:07,146 --> 00:04:07,646
frontier.

101
00:04:08,146 --> 00:04:11,026
By Stalin's first rule, it could never qualify.

102
00:04:11,526 --> 00:04:14,167
Stalin himself used the Tatar and Bashkir

103
00:04:14,207 --> 00:04:15,667
republics as his examples,

104
00:04:16,067 --> 00:04:18,047
and the same logic applied to Chuvashia

105
00:04:18,467 --> 00:04:20,528
and the other republics of the Volga and

106
00:04:20,648 --> 00:04:21,408
Ural regions.

107
00:04:21,988 --> 00:04:24,368
In practice, Moscow applied these rules

108
00:04:24,588 --> 00:04:25,629
when it suited Moscow.

109
00:04:26,009 --> 00:04:28,609
Kazakhstan was promoted even though Kazakhs

110
00:04:28,809 --> 00:04:30,889
no longer made up a majority of their own

111
00:04:30,929 --> 00:04:31,409
republic.

112
00:04:31,690 --> 00:04:33,170
But the principle was set.

113
00:04:34,050 --> 00:04:37,551
Geography, not identity, decided which nations

114
00:04:37,631 --> 00:04:38,551
got the top rank.

115
00:04:38,871 --> 00:04:40,272
The map kept moving.

116
00:04:40,712 --> 00:04:44,093
In 1940, the Soviet Union annexed Estonia,

117
00:04:44,393 --> 00:04:47,374
Latvia and Lithuania, and created the Moldavian

118
00:04:47,414 --> 00:04:50,095
republic from territory taken from Romania,

119
00:04:50,495 --> 00:04:53,036
joined with a strip of Ukrainian land east

120
00:04:53,076 --> 00:04:53,796
of the Dniester.

121
00:04:54,276 --> 00:04:56,677
It also created a Karelo-Finnish republic

122
00:04:56,877 --> 00:04:58,017
on the border with Finland,

123
00:04:58,357 --> 00:05:00,818
a republic designed to absorb Finland if

124
00:05:00,858 --> 00:05:01,498
it ever fell.

125
00:05:01,878 --> 00:05:03,819
That brought the total to sixteen.

126
00:05:04,279 --> 00:05:07,200
But Finland never fell, and Finns made up

127
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:09,841
only a small minority of the new republic.

128
00:05:10,381 --> 00:05:14,102
So in 1956, Moscow quietly downgraded it

129
00:05:14,302 --> 00:05:16,783
to an autonomous republic inside Russia.

130
00:05:17,523 --> 00:05:20,984
Sixteen became fifteen, and the number never

131
00:05:21,284 --> 00:05:21,984
changed again.

132
00:05:22,544 --> 00:05:25,105
Two years earlier, in 1954,

133
00:05:25,605 --> 00:05:27,526
another quiet decision had been made.

134
00:05:27,946 --> 00:05:30,207
The Crimean peninsula was transferred from

135
00:05:30,227 --> 00:05:32,367
the Russian republic to the Ukrainian republic.

136
00:05:32,788 --> 00:05:35,008
At the time, it seemed like an administrative

137
00:05:35,028 --> 00:05:35,468
detail.

138
00:05:35,948 --> 00:05:38,349
Nobody imagined that these internal lines

139
00:05:38,469 --> 00:05:40,870
would one day become international borders.

140
00:05:41,790 --> 00:05:45,151
In the late 1980s, Mikhail Gorbachev loosened

141
00:05:45,191 --> 00:05:46,031
the grip of fear.

142
00:05:47,031 --> 00:05:49,811
Suddenly, the old promises written in the

143
00:05:49,851 --> 00:05:51,812
constitution started to matter.

144
00:05:52,352 --> 00:05:55,632
In August 1989, some two million people

145
00:05:55,692 --> 00:05:58,253
joined hands in a human chain stretching

146
00:05:58,293 --> 00:06:01,273
across Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania.

147
00:06:01,753 --> 00:06:05,274
On March 11th, 1990, Lithuania declared

148
00:06:05,314 --> 00:06:07,454
that it was restoring its independence.

149
00:06:07,994 --> 00:06:10,295
Moscow responded with a law that made leaving

150
00:06:10,435 --> 00:06:13,035
extremely difficult, demanding referendums,

151
00:06:13,315 --> 00:06:15,376
two-thirds majorities and a transition of

152
00:06:15,416 --> 00:06:16,316
up to five years.

153
00:06:16,756 --> 00:06:20,317
In March 1991, the Kremlin held a referendum

154
00:06:20,437 --> 00:06:21,577
on preserving the Union.

155
00:06:22,217 --> 00:06:24,177
Six republics refused to take part:

156
00:06:24,677 --> 00:06:26,758
the three Baltic states, Georgia,

157
00:06:27,178 --> 00:06:28,578
Armenia and Moldova.

158
00:06:29,118 --> 00:06:32,659
Then, in August 1991, hardliners tried to

159
00:06:32,759 --> 00:06:33,979
seize power in Moscow.

160
00:06:34,399 --> 00:06:36,039
The coup collapsed in three days,

161
00:06:36,459 --> 00:06:38,140
and so did the authority of the center.

162
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:41,900
Within weeks, republic after republic declared

163
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:42,760
independence.

164
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:46,621
In September, Moscow recognized the independence

165
00:06:46,881 --> 00:06:47,941
of the Baltic states.

166
00:06:48,461 --> 00:06:50,802
On December 1st, more than ninety percent

167
00:06:50,922 --> 00:06:53,502
of Ukrainian voters chose independence.

168
00:06:54,102 --> 00:06:56,143
A week later, the leaders of Russia,

169
00:06:56,643 --> 00:06:59,583
Ukraine and Belarus met in a hunting lodge

170
00:06:59,923 --> 00:07:02,544
and declared that the Soviet Union no longer

171
00:07:02,664 --> 00:07:03,184
existed.

172
00:07:03,744 --> 00:07:06,824
On December 21st, eleven republics confirmed

173
00:07:06,884 --> 00:07:06,964
it.

174
00:07:07,584 --> 00:07:09,885
Four days later, Gorbachev resigned.

175
00:07:10,325 --> 00:07:11,965
The fifteen union republics,

176
00:07:12,205 --> 00:07:13,906
the outer shells of the matryoshka,

177
00:07:14,306 --> 00:07:16,986
stepped onto the world stage as fifteen

178
00:07:17,226 --> 00:07:18,286
independent states.

179
00:07:19,226 --> 00:07:20,866
But what about the dolls inside?

180
00:07:21,306 --> 00:07:22,106
They tried too.

181
00:07:22,926 --> 00:07:25,927
Tatarstan declared its sovereignty in 1990,

182
00:07:26,587 --> 00:07:30,467
and in 1992 about sixty-one percent of voters

183
00:07:30,827 --> 00:07:32,267
backed a sovereign status.

184
00:07:32,867 --> 00:07:35,168
But with no foreign border and Russia on

185
00:07:35,248 --> 00:07:37,548
every side, it settled for a special treaty

186
00:07:37,628 --> 00:07:39,688
with Moscow in 1994.

187
00:07:40,588 --> 00:07:41,888
Chechnya went further.

188
00:07:42,908 --> 00:07:46,509
In November 1991, it declared full independence.

189
00:07:46,889 --> 00:07:49,069
The result was two devastating wars,

190
00:07:49,309 --> 00:07:55,930
from 1994 to 1996, and again from 1999,

191
00:07:56,210 --> 00:07:58,830
and the second ended with Chechnya firmly

192
00:07:59,150 --> 00:08:00,410
under Moscow's control.

193
00:08:00,970 --> 00:08:04,170
Abkhazia, South Ossetia and Transnistria

194
00:08:04,490 --> 00:08:06,691
also broke away from the new states around

195
00:08:06,711 --> 00:08:09,611
them, leading to bloody conflicts and decades

196
00:08:09,671 --> 00:08:10,691
of frozen disputes.

197
00:08:11,211 --> 00:08:12,711
Nagorno-Karabakh did too,

198
00:08:13,071 --> 00:08:15,771
but in 2023 Azerbaijan retook it,

199
00:08:16,112 --> 00:08:18,552
and its self-declared republic was dissolved.

200
00:08:18,892 --> 00:08:21,632
None of them won broad international recognition.

201
00:08:22,052 --> 00:08:22,332
Why?

202
00:08:22,752 --> 00:08:24,612
When the new states formed their Commonwealth

203
00:08:24,732 --> 00:08:27,433
in December 1991, they pledged to respect

204
00:08:27,493 --> 00:08:30,033
each other's territorial integrity and existing

205
00:08:30,073 --> 00:08:30,453
borders.

206
00:08:30,933 --> 00:08:33,253
And the wider world followed an old legal

207
00:08:33,313 --> 00:08:36,173
principle called uti possidetis juris,

208
00:08:36,553 --> 00:08:38,814
which means that new states inherit the

209
00:08:38,854 --> 00:08:41,354
borders they held as administrative units.

210
00:08:41,874 --> 00:08:44,454
The fifteen union republics had legal borders

211
00:08:44,714 --> 00:08:46,554
and a constitutional right to leave.

212
00:08:46,914 --> 00:08:49,195
The units inside them had no right to leave

213
00:08:49,315 --> 00:08:49,815
on their own.

214
00:08:50,275 --> 00:08:53,295
So the lines drawn by Soviet planners became

215
00:08:53,315 --> 00:08:55,415
the borders of the post-Soviet world.

216
00:08:56,316 --> 00:08:58,797
In the end, the Soviet Union did not break

217
00:08:58,857 --> 00:09:00,757
apart along the lines of language,

218
00:09:01,017 --> 00:09:02,198
religion or history.

219
00:09:02,678 --> 00:09:05,519
It broke along the seams that its own founders

220
00:09:05,799 --> 00:09:06,699
had stitched into it.

221
00:09:07,199 --> 00:09:09,680
Lenin and Stalin built a system where the

222
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:12,721
right to independence existed only on paper,

223
00:09:13,041 --> 00:09:15,382
and in practice only for nations on the

224
00:09:15,482 --> 00:09:16,122
edge of the map.

225
00:09:16,442 --> 00:09:18,863
They never imagined that paper would one

226
00:09:18,903 --> 00:09:19,983
day be enough.

227
00:09:20,603 --> 00:09:22,924
When the moment came, only the outermost

228
00:09:22,964 --> 00:09:24,144
dolls could walk away.

229
00:09:24,624 --> 00:09:28,245
And that is why one superpower became exactly

230
00:09:28,705 --> 00:09:32,006
fifteen countries, no more and no less.

